er\nAuthors: Eddie Kohler, Robert Morris, Benjie Chen, John Jannotti, and M. Frans Kaashoek\n\nAbstract\nClick is a flexible software architecture for building modular routers using packet processing elements. A router is composed of elements arranged in a directed graph, allowing for powerful processing pipelines. Click achieves good performance, reaching 333,000 packets/sec, and supports standards-compliant IP routing, complex queueing, and extensions like Differentiated Services.\n\n1. Introduction\nTraditional routers are static and inflexible. Click introduces modularity through elements representing small router functions (e.g., classification, queueing). These can be connected in various ways to build complex, extensible routers on general-purpose hardware.\n\n2. Architecture\nClick routers are graphs of elements. Key features:\n- Push and pull connections model asynchronous packet flows.\n- Explicit queue elements allow fine-grained control over buffering.\n- Flow-based router context helps elements locate others for interaction.\n- A declarative configuration language defines routers.\n- Kernel/user-level drivers allow deployment and testing.\n\n3. An IP Router\nA complete IP router is implemented using 16 elements per path. Functions include TTL decrementing, IP fragmentation, ARP querying, and ICMP error generation. Annotations carry metadata across elements (e.g., destination IP, TTL). The router is modular, extensible, and standards-compliant.\n\n4. Extensions\nClick supports:\n- Packet scheduling (e.g., round-robin, priority schedulers)\n- RED dropping policies with flexible queue handling\n- Weighted RED and stochastic fairness queueing\n- IP tunneling, Differentiated Services, and mobile IP\n- An Ethernet switch using IEEE 802.1d spanning tree\n\n5. Kernel Environment\nClick runs as a kernel thread with polling-based device drivers for high performance. Packet I/O uses DMA queues. User interaction is handled through `/proc/click`. Elements are lightweight C++ objects, easily implemented and extended.\n\n6. Evaluation\nTests show that Click achieves high throughput (333K pps), efficient CPU usage, and flexible configuration with low overhead. Modularity overhead is minor (about 1µs for 16 elements). Custom elements can reduce function call costs. Differentiated Services are enforced effectively even under load.\n\n7. Related Work\nClick is compared with x-kernel, Scout, STREAMS, Netgraph, ALTQ, and router plugin systems. Click is unique in its modular, extensible router-focused design. Prior work focused on protocol stacks or fixed-function routers.\n\n8. Conclusion\nClick is an open and extensible software router architecture that combines modularity with high performance. Its flexible design supports complex router functions and is suited for research, deployment, and active networking.\n\nAppendix: Element Glossary\nThe appendix lists 38 commonly used elements like `FromDevice`, `Queue`, `ARPQuerier`, `Classifier`, `Tee`, `RED`, and `ToDevice`, describing their roles and configurations.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.003959503024816513}, {'seq_length': 38, 'latency': 1.6237264340743423, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: How does the approach ensure the correctness of the composition process?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: The paper "Formalizing Compound MAPE Patterns for Decentralized Control in Self-adaptive Systems" by Hachicha et al. addresses the challenge of engineering decentralized self-adaptive systems by proposing a formal approach to compose multiple MAPE (Monitor, Analyze, Plan, Execute) control loop patterns. The motivation stems from the limitations of centralized adaptation logic—such as single points of failure and scalability issues—in large and dynamic systems, making decentralized and hybrid control patterns essential.\n\nBackground and Motivation\n\nSelf-adaptive systems autonomously adjust their behavior in response to changes in their environment or internal state. The MAPE loop is a widely adopted architectural pattern for enabling such adaptation. However, real-world systems often require more than a single MAPE loop, necessitating the use of compound patterns that combine multiple adaptation strategies (e.g., master/slave, coordinated control). Prior work identified various MAPE patterns with different decentralization degrees, but did not provide a formal mechanism for their composition.\n\nPattern Composition Approach\n\nThe authors introduce a two-step composition approach:\n\nDescription: Compound MAPE patterns are described as sets of unit patterns linked via merging operators.\n\nConcretization: Merging operators are applied to form the compound pattern.\n\nTwo merging operators are defined:\n\nStringing operator: Connects components from different patterns (either of the same or compatible types), resulting in new communication links.\n\nOverlapping operator: Merges components of the same type from different patterns into a single component, preserving their interfaces and connections.\n\nThese operators enable flexible composition, allowing designers to tailor adaptation architectures to complex requirements by combining the strengths of different patterns.\n\nFormalization with Event-B\n\nTo ensure the correctness and rigor of the composition process, the approach is formalized using the Event-B method:\n\nStructural features (components, connections) are specified using contexts in Event-B, which extend the contexts of the constituent patterns and formalize the effects of merging operators.\n\nBehavioral features (adaptation logic, event synchronization) are specified using machines, with parallel composition of sub-machines representing the behavior of the compound pattern. Shared events synchronize the operation of merged components, ensuring consistent adaptation logic.\n\nCase Study: Forest Fire Detection System\n\nThe approach is illustrated with a forest fire detection system based on wireless sensor networks. Here, a hybrid MAPE pattern is constructed by composing the master/slave and coordinated control patterns:\n\nThe master/slave pattern handles hierarchical adaptation (e.g., cluster heads and base stations).\n\nThe coordinated control pattern enables peer-to-peer adaptation among sensor nodes.\n\nThe composition leverages overlapping and stringing operators to merge and connect components, achieving both robustness (avoiding single points of failure) and efficient coordination.\n\nRelated Work and Contributions\n\nWhile previous works have cataloged MAPE patterns or proposed semi-formal composition approaches (e.g., using UML), this paper is novel in providing a formal, Event-B-based method for specifying and verifying compound MAPE patterns. This allows for precise reasoning about both structure and behavior, facilitating the design of correct and robust self-adaptive systems.\n\nConclusion and Outlook\n\nThe paper demonstrates that formal composition of MAPE patterns is feasible and beneficial for decentralized self-adaptive systems. By using Event-B, both the structure and the dynamic behavior of compound adaptation architectures can be rigorously specified and analyzed. Future work aims to extend this formalization to verify properties such as safety and liveness for complex compound patterns'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.01846272498369217}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 1.186953610740602, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which two MAPE patterns are composed in the forest fire detection system example?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: The paper "Formalizing Compound MAPE Patterns for Decentralized Control in Self-adaptive Systems" by Hachicha et al. addresses the challenge of engineering decentralized self-adaptive systems by proposing a formal approach to compose multiple MAPE (Monitor, Analyze, Plan, Execute) control loop patterns. The motivation stems from the limitations of centralized adaptation logic—such as single points of failure and scalability issues—in large and dynamic systems, making decentralized and hybrid control patterns essential.\n\nBackground and Motivation\n\nSelf-adaptive systems autonomously adjust their behavior in response to changes in their environment or internal state. The MAPE loop is a widely adopted architectural pattern for enabling such adaptation. However, real-world systems often require more than a single MAPE loop, necessitating the use of compound patterns that combine multiple adaptation strategies (e.g., master/slave, coordinated control). Prior work identified various MAPE patterns with different decentralization degrees, but did not provide a formal mechanism for their composition.\n\nPattern Composition Approach\n\nThe authors introduce a two-step composition approach:\n\nDescription: Compound MAPE patterns are described as sets of unit patterns linked via merging operators.\n\nConcretization: Merging operators are applied to form the compound pattern.\n\nTwo merging operators are defined:\n\nStringing operator: Connects components from different patterns (either of the same or compatible types), resulting in new communication links.\n\nOverlapping operator: Merges components of the same type from different patterns into a single component, preserving their interfaces and connections.\n\nThese operators enable flexible composition, allowing designers to tailor adaptation architectures to complex requirements by combining the strengths of different patterns.\n\nFormalization with Event-B\n\nTo ensure the correctness and rigor of the composition process, the approach is formalized using the Event-B method:\n\nStructural features (components, connections) are specified using contexts in Event-B, which extend the contexts of the constituent patterns and formalize the effects of merging operators.\n\nBehavioral features (adaptation logic, event synchronization) are specified using machines, with parallel composition of sub-machines representing the behavior of the compound pattern. Shared events synchronize the operation of merged components, ensuring consistent adaptation logic.\n\nCase Study: Forest Fire Detection System\n\nThe approach is illustrated with a forest fire detection system based on wireless sensor networks. Here, a hybrid MAPE pattern is constructed by composing the master/slave and coordinated control patterns:\n\nThe master/slave pattern handles hierarchical adaptation (e.g., cluster heads and base stations).\n\nThe coordinated control pattern enables peer-to-peer adaptation among sensor nodes.\n\nThe composition leverages overlapping and stringing operators to merge and connect components, achieving both robustness (avoiding single points of failure) and efficient coordination.\n\nRelated Work and Contributions\n\nWhile previous works have cataloged MAPE patterns or proposed semi-formal composition approaches (e.g., using UML), this paper is novel in providing a formal, Event-B-based method for specifying and verifying compound MAPE patterns. This allows for precise reasoning about both structure and behavior, facilitating the design of correct and robust self-adaptive systems.\n\nConclusion and Outlook\n\nThe paper demonstrates that formal composition of MAPE patterns is feasible and beneficial for decentralized self-adaptive systems. By using Event-B, both the structure and the dynamic behavior of compound adaptation architectures can be rigorously specified and analyzed. Future work aims to extend this formalization to verify properties such as safety and liveness for complex compound patterns'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004016711376607418}, {'seq_length': 43, 'latency': 0.46485050953924656, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What are the two main steps in the proposed approach for composing compound MAPE patterns?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: The paper "Formalizing Compound MAPE Patterns for Decentralized Control in Self-adaptive Systems" by Hachicha et al. addresses the challenge of engineering decentralized self-adaptive systems by proposing a formal approach to compose multiple MAPE (Monitor, Analyze, Plan, Execute) control loop patterns. The motivation stems from the limitations of centralized adaptation logic—such as single points of failure and scalability issues—in large and dynamic systems, making decentralized and hybrid control patterns essential.\n\nBackground and Motivation\n\nSelf-adaptive systems autonomously adjust their behavior in response to changes in their environment or internal state. The MAPE loop is a widely adopted architectural pattern for enabling such adaptation. However, real-world systems often require more than a single MAPE loop, necessitating the use of compound patterns that combine multiple adaptation strategies (e.g., master/slave, coordinated control). Prior work identified various MAPE patterns with different decentralization degrees, but did not provide a formal mechanism for their composition.\n\nPattern Composition Approach\n\nThe authors introduce a two-step composition approach:\n\nDescription: Compound MAPE patterns are described as sets of unit patterns linked via merging operators.\n\nConcretization: Merging operators are applied to form the compound pattern.\n\nTwo merging operators are defined:\n\nStringing operator: Connects components from different patterns (either of the same or compatible types), resulting in new communication links.\n\nOverlapping operator: Merges components of the same type from different patterns into a single component, preserving their interfaces and connections.\n\nThese operators enable flexible composition, allowing designers to tailor adaptation architectures to complex requirements by combining the strengths of different patterns.\n\nFormalization with Event-B\n\nTo ensure the correctness and rigor of the composition process, the approach is formalized using the Event-B method:\n\nStructural features (components, connections) are specified using contexts in Event-B, which extend the contexts of the constituent patterns and formalize the effects of merging operators.\n\nBehavioral features (adaptation logic, event synchronization) are specified using machines, with parallel composition of sub-machines representing the behavior of the compound pattern. Shared events synchronize the operation of merged components, ensuring consistent adaptation logic.\n\nCase Study: Forest Fire Detection System\n\nThe approach is illustrated with a forest fire detection system based on wireless sensor networks. Here, a hybrid MAPE pattern is constructed by composing the master/slave and coordinated control patterns:\n\nThe master/slave pattern handles hierarchical adaptation (e.g., cluster heads and base stations).\n\nThe coordinated control pattern enables peer-to-peer adaptation among sensor nodes.\n\nThe composition leverages overlapping and stringing operators to merge and connect components, achieving both robustness (avoiding single points of failure) and efficient coordination.\n\nRelated Work and Contributions\n\nWhile previous works have cataloged MAPE patterns or proposed semi-formal composition approaches (e.g., using UML), this paper is novel in providing a formal, Event-B-based method for specifying and verifying compound MAPE patterns. This allows for precise reasoning about both structure and behavior, facilitating the design of correct and robust self-adaptive systems.\n\nConclusion and Outlook\n\nThe paper demonstrates that formal composition of MAPE patterns is feasible and beneficial for decentralized self-adaptive systems. By using Event-B, both the structure and the dynamic behavior of compound adaptation architectures can be rigorously specified and analyzed. Future work aims to extend this formalization to verify properties such as safety and liveness for complex compound patterns'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004026411101222038}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 0.42909739818423986, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What future improvements do the authors propose for the PAPE methodology?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Problem Context and Motivation\n\nMachine learning models often experience performance degradation after deployment due to covariate shift—changes in the distribution of input data over time. In production environments, true labels are frequently unavailable or significantly delayed, making it difficult to assess whether a model continues to perform as expected. Existing proxy methods, such as drift detection, typically only monitor changes in input data and do not directly quantify the impact of these shifts on model performance. As a result, organizations risk undetected model failures and potential financial losses.\n\nMain Contribution: The PAPE Algorithm\n\nThe authors propose Probabilistic Adaptive Performance Estimation (PAPE), a novel, model- and data-type-agnostic algorithm for estimating the performance of classification models on unlabeled data under covariate shift. PAPE:\n\nRequires only model predictions and probability estimates, not access to the model internals or assumptions about the nature of the shift.\n\nLearns directly from data, adapting to shifts without user intervention.\n\nEstimates a wide range of performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, AUROC, F1) and is theoretically grounded.\n\nAlgorithm Overview\n\nPAPE operates in the following steps:\n\nDensity Ratio Estimation: It estimates the ratio between the probability densities of the production and reference (labeled) data using a classifier to distinguish between them. This ratio is used to reweight the reference data to resemble the production distribution.\n\nWeighted Calibration: A regressor is trained on the reference data, using the estimated density ratios as weights, to calibrate the model’s probability scores.\n\nPerformance Metric Estimation: The calibrated probabilities are used to compute the expected value of the desired performance metric on the production data, without requiring true labels.\n\nThe core theoretical guarantee is that, under the assumption that the conditional label distribution given the input remains stable (\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\nr\ne\nf\n=\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np\nr\no\nd\np(y∣x) \nref\n =p(y∣x) \nprod\n ), the PAPE estimate converges to the true performance as calibration improves and coverage is sufficient.\n\nEvaluation and Results\n\nThe authors evaluated PAPE on 959 model-dataset pairs derived from US Census data (Folktables), covering five classification tasks and five popular model types (e.g., logistic regression, neural networks, tree ensembles). They compared PAPE against several existing methods, including:\n\nImportance Weighting (IW)\n\nConfidence-Based Performance Estimation (CBPE)\n\nAverage Threshold Confidence (ATC)\n\nDifference of Confidence (DoC)\n\nReverse Testing (RT-mod)\n\nTest Set Performance Baseline\n\nPerformance was assessed using scaled error metrics (MASTE, RMSSTE) to account for varying performance stability across cases. PAPE consistently achieved the lowest estimation errors across all metrics (accuracy, AUROC, F1), outperforming all baselines. For example, PAPE’s MASTE for accuracy was 0.97, compared to 1.62 for the test set baseline and 1.04 for IW. The advantage was most pronounced in cases with significant performance changes.\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n\nAssumption of Stable Conditional Label Distribution: PAPE relies on \np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np(y∣x) being unchanged between reference and production data. If the production data contains regions not covered by the reference set, estimation quality degrades.\n\nCalibration Quality: The method’s accuracy depends on the quality of the probability calibration, which can be challenging on finite samples.\n\nExtension to Other Data Types: While PAPE is data-type agnostic in principle, density ratio estimation may require adaptation for non-tabular data.\n\nThe authors plan to extend their work by quantifying estimation uncertainties, improving calibration (e.g., via cross-validation and Bayesian approaches), and expanding evaluation to more diverse datasets.\n\nConclusion\n\nPAPE provides a practical, accurate, and theoretically justified approach for estimating model performance under covariate shift without labels, addressing a critical gap in real-world machine learning monitoring. It enables organizations to detect and quantify performance degradation in production, supporting timely interventions and risk mitigation'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004009939730167389}, {'seq_length': 40, 'latency': 0.5962076736614108, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which baseline performance estimation methods were compared against PAPE in the experiments?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Problem Context and Motivation\n\nMachine learning models often experience performance degradation after deployment due to covariate shift—changes in the distribution of input data over time. In production environments, true labels are frequently unavailable or significantly delayed, making it difficult to assess whether a model continues to perform as expected. Existing proxy methods, such as drift detection, typically only monitor changes in input data and do not directly quantify the impact of these shifts on model performance. As a result, organizations risk undetected model failures and potential financial losses.\n\nMain Contribution: The PAPE Algorithm\n\nThe authors propose Probabilistic Adaptive Performance Estimation (PAPE), a novel, model- and data-type-agnostic algorithm for estimating the performance of classification models on unlabeled data under covariate shift. PAPE:\n\nRequires only model predictions and probability estimates, not access to the model internals or assumptions about the nature of the shift.\n\nLearns directly from data, adapting to shifts without user intervention.\n\nEstimates a wide range of performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, AUROC, F1) and is theoretically grounded.\n\nAlgorithm Overview\n\nPAPE operates in the following steps:\n\nDensity Ratio Estimation: It estimates the ratio between the probability densities of the production and reference (labeled) data using a classifier to distinguish between them. This ratio is used to reweight the reference data to resemble the production distribution.\n\nWeighted Calibration: A regressor is trained on the reference data, using the estimated density ratios as weights, to calibrate the model’s probability scores.\n\nPerformance Metric Estimation: The calibrated probabilities are used to compute the expected value of the desired performance metric on the production data, without requiring true labels.\n\nThe core theoretical guarantee is that, under the assumption that the conditional label distribution given the input remains stable (\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\nr\ne\nf\n=\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np\nr\no\nd\np(y∣x) \nref\n =p(y∣x) \nprod\n ), the PAPE estimate converges to the true performance as calibration improves and coverage is sufficient.\n\nEvaluation and Results\n\nThe authors evaluated PAPE on 959 model-dataset pairs derived from US Census data (Folktables), covering five classification tasks and five popular model types (e.g., logistic regression, neural networks, tree ensembles). They compared PAPE against several existing methods, including:\n\nImportance Weighting (IW)\n\nConfidence-Based Performance Estimation (CBPE)\n\nAverage Threshold Confidence (ATC)\n\nDifference of Confidence (DoC)\n\nReverse Testing (RT-mod)\n\nTest Set Performance Baseline\n\nPerformance was assessed using scaled error metrics (MASTE, RMSSTE) to account for varying performance stability across cases. PAPE consistently achieved the lowest estimation errors across all metrics (accuracy, AUROC, F1), outperforming all baselines. For example, PAPE’s MASTE for accuracy was 0.97, compared to 1.62 for the test set baseline and 1.04 for IW. The advantage was most pronounced in cases with significant performance changes.\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n\nAssumption of Stable Conditional Label Distribution: PAPE relies on \np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np(y∣x) being unchanged between reference and production data. If the production data contains regions not covered by the reference set, estimation quality degrades.\n\nCalibration Quality: The method’s accuracy depends on the quality of the probability calibration, which can be challenging on finite samples.\n\nExtension to Other Data Types: While PAPE is data-type agnostic in principle, density ratio estimation may require adaptation for non-tabular data.\n\nThe authors plan to extend their work by quantifying estimation uncertainties, improving calibration (e.g., via cross-validation and Bayesian approaches), and expanding evaluation to more diverse datasets.\n\nConclusion\n\nPAPE provides a practical, accurate, and theoretically justified approach for estimating model performance under covariate shift without labels, addressing a critical gap in real-world machine learning monitoring. It enables organizations to detect and quantify performance degradation in production, supporting timely interventions and risk mitigation'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004030832089483738}, {'seq_length': 43, 'latency': 0.6275975601747632, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What limitations of PAPE are discussed in the paper regarding coverage of the reference distribution?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Problem Context and Motivation\n\nMachine learning models often experience performance degradation after deployment due to covariate shift—changes in the distribution of input data over time. In production environments, true labels are frequently unavailable or significantly delayed, making it difficult to assess whether a model continues to perform as expected. Existing proxy methods, such as drift detection, typically only monitor changes in input data and do not directly quantify the impact of these shifts on model performance. As a result, organizations risk undetected model failures and potential financial losses.\n\nMain Contribution: The PAPE Algorithm\n\nThe authors propose Probabilistic Adaptive Performance Estimation (PAPE), a novel, model- and data-type-agnostic algorithm for estimating the performance of classification models on unlabeled data under covariate shift. PAPE:\n\nRequires only model predictions and probability estimates, not access to the model internals or assumptions about the nature of the shift.\n\nLearns directly from data, adapting to shifts without user intervention.\n\nEstimates a wide range of performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, AUROC, F1) and is theoretically grounded.\n\nAlgorithm Overview\n\nPAPE operates in the following steps:\n\nDensity Ratio Estimation: It estimates the ratio between the probability densities of the production and reference (labeled) data using a classifier to distinguish between them. This ratio is used to reweight the reference data to resemble the production distribution.\n\nWeighted Calibration: A regressor is trained on the reference data, using the estimated density ratios as weights, to calibrate the model’s probability scores.\n\nPerformance Metric Estimation: The calibrated probabilities are used to compute the expected value of the desired performance metric on the production data, without requiring true labels.\n\nThe core theoretical guarantee is that, under the assumption that the conditional label distribution given the input remains stable (\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\nr\ne\nf\n=\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np\nr\no\nd\np(y∣x) \nref\n =p(y∣x) \nprod\n ), the PAPE estimate converges to the true performance as calibration improves and coverage is sufficient.\n\nEvaluation and Results\n\nThe authors evaluated PAPE on 959 model-dataset pairs derived from US Census data (Folktables), covering five classification tasks and five popular model types (e.g., logistic regression, neural networks, tree ensembles). They compared PAPE against several existing methods, including:\n\nImportance Weighting (IW)\n\nConfidence-Based Performance Estimation (CBPE)\n\nAverage Threshold Confidence (ATC)\n\nDifference of Confidence (DoC)\n\nReverse Testing (RT-mod)\n\nTest Set Performance Baseline\n\nPerformance was assessed using scaled error metrics (MASTE, RMSSTE) to account for varying performance stability across cases. PAPE consistently achieved the lowest estimation errors across all metrics (accuracy, AUROC, F1), outperforming all baselines. For example, PAPE’s MASTE for accuracy was 0.97, compared to 1.62 for the test set baseline and 1.04 for IW. The advantage was most pronounced in cases with significant performance changes.\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n\nAssumption of Stable Conditional Label Distribution: PAPE relies on \np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np(y∣x) being unchanged between reference and production data. If the production data contains regions not covered by the reference set, estimation quality degrades.\n\nCalibration Quality: The method’s accuracy depends on the quality of the probability calibration, which can be challenging on finite samples.\n\nExtension to Other Data Types: While PAPE is data-type agnostic in principle, density ratio estimation may require adaptation for non-tabular data.\n\nThe authors plan to extend their work by quantifying estimation uncertainties, improving calibration (e.g., via cross-validation and Bayesian approaches), and expanding evaluation to more diverse datasets.\n\nConclusion\n\nPAPE provides a practical, accurate, and theoretically justified approach for estimating model performance under covariate shift without labels, addressing a critical gap in real-world machine learning monitoring. It enables organizations to detect and quantify performance degradation in production, supporting timely interventions and risk mitigation'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.0040481602773070335}, {'seq_length': 38, 'latency': 0.969063431955874, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What variables are analyzed in UC2 (Energy Consumption Prediction)?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Problem Context and Motivation\n\nMachine learning models often experience performance degradation after deployment due to covariate shift—changes in the distribution of input data over time. In production environments, true labels are frequently unavailable or significantly delayed, making it difficult to assess whether a model continues to perform as expected. Existing proxy methods, such as drift detection, typically only monitor changes in input data and do not directly quantify the impact of these shifts on model performance. As a result, organizations risk undetected model failures and potential financial losses.\n\nMain Contribution: The PAPE Algorithm\n\nThe authors propose Probabilistic Adaptive Performance Estimation (PAPE), a novel, model- and data-type-agnostic algorithm for estimating the performance of classification models on unlabeled data under covariate shift. PAPE:\n\nRequires only model predictions and probability estimates, not access to the model internals or assumptions about the nature of the shift.\n\nLearns directly from data, adapting to shifts without user intervention.\n\nEstimates a wide range of performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, AUROC, F1) and is theoretically grounded.\n\nAlgorithm Overview\n\nPAPE operates in the following steps:\n\nDensity Ratio Estimation: It estimates the ratio between the probability densities of the production and reference (labeled) data using a classifier to distinguish between them. This ratio is used to reweight the reference data to resemble the production distribution.\n\nWeighted Calibration: A regressor is trained on the reference data, using the estimated density ratios as weights, to calibrate the model’s probability scores.\n\nPerformance Metric Estimation: The calibrated probabilities are used to compute the expected value of the desired performance metric on the production data, without requiring true labels.\n\nThe core theoretical guarantee is that, under the assumption that the conditional label distribution given the input remains stable (\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\nr\ne\nf\n=\np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np\nr\no\nd\np(y∣x) \nref\n =p(y∣x) \nprod\n ), the PAPE estimate converges to the true performance as calibration improves and coverage is sufficient.\n\nEvaluation and Results\n\nThe authors evaluated PAPE on 959 model-dataset pairs derived from US Census data (Folktables), covering five classification tasks and five popular model types (e.g., logistic regression, neural networks, tree ensembles). They compared PAPE against several existing methods, including:\n\nImportance Weighting (IW)\n\nConfidence-Based Performance Estimation (CBPE)\n\nAverage Threshold Confidence (ATC)\n\nDifference of Confidence (DoC)\n\nReverse Testing (RT-mod)\n\nTest Set Performance Baseline\n\nPerformance was assessed using scaled error metrics (MASTE, RMSSTE) to account for varying performance stability across cases. PAPE consistently achieved the lowest estimation errors across all metrics (accuracy, AUROC, F1), outperforming all baselines. For example, PAPE’s MASTE for accuracy was 0.97, compared to 1.62 for the test set baseline and 1.04 for IW. The advantage was most pronounced in cases with significant performance changes.\n\nLimitations and Future Work\n\nAssumption of Stable Conditional Label Distribution: PAPE relies on \np\n(\ny\n∣\nx\n)\np(y∣x) being unchanged between reference and production data. If the production data contains regions not covered by the reference set, estimation quality degrades.\n\nCalibration Quality: The method’s accuracy depends on the quality of the probability calibration, which can be challenging on finite samples.\n\nExtension to Other Data Types: While PAPE is data-type agnostic in principle, density ratio estimation may require adaptation for non-tabular data.\n\nThe authors plan to extend their work by quantifying estimation uncertainties, improving calibration (e.g., via cross-validation and Bayesian approaches), and expanding evaluation to more diverse datasets.\n\nConclusion\n\nPAPE provides a practical, accurate, and theoretically justified approach for estimating model performance under covariate shift without labels, addressing a critical gap in real-world machine learning monitoring. It enables organizations to detect and quantify performance degradation in production, supporting timely interventions and risk mitigation'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004009728319942951}, {'seq_length': 38, 'latency': 0.3684425409883261, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: How does digital banking make money management more efficient and accessible?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report: The Rise of Digital Banking in Personal Finance\nIntroduction\nThe financial landscape has undergone a profound transformation in the last decade, with digital banking emerging as a dominant force in personal finance management. This report explores the key drivers, benefits, and challenges of digital banking, and its impact on individuals’ financial habits.\n\nKey Drivers of Digital Banking Adoption\nTechnological Advancements\nThe proliferation of smartphones and high-speed internet has made banking services accessible anytime, anywhere. Enhanced security protocols, such as biometric authentication, have also increased user trust.\n\nChanging Consumer Expectations\nModern consumers demand convenience, speed, and transparency. Digital banks offer seamless user experiences, intuitive interfaces, and real-time notifications, aligning with these expectations.\n\nCost Efficiency\nDigital-only banks operate without physical branches, reducing overhead costs. These savings are often passed on to customers through lower fees and higher interest rates on deposits.\n\nBenefits for Personal Finance\n24/7 Access and Control\nUsers can check balances, transfer funds, and pay bills instantly, empowering them to manage their finances proactively.\n\nPersonalized Insights\nMany digital banks use AI to analyze spending patterns and provide tailored budgeting tips, helping users save more effectively.\n\nEnhanced Security\nFeatures like two-factor authentication, instant card freezing, and transaction alerts improve security and reduce fraud risk.\n\nChallenges and Considerations\nCybersecurity Risks\nAs digital banking grows, so do threats from hackers and scammers. Continuous investment in cybersecurity is essential.\n\nDigital Divide\nNot all populations have equal access to digital tools, potentially excluding vulnerable groups from the benefits of digital banking.\n\nRegulatory Compliance\nDigital banks must navigate complex regulations across different jurisdictions, balancing innovation with compliance.\n\nImpact on Financial Habits\nDigital banking encourages financial literacy by providing users with real-time data and educational resources. Automated savings features and spending categorizations help users set and achieve financial goals, fostering healthier financial behaviors.\n\nConclusion\nDigital banking is reshaping personal finance by making money management more accessible, efficient, and secure. While challenges remain, its continued evolution promises to further empower individuals in their financial journeys.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.0042730662971735}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 1.102181433700025, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Name two technological advancements that have driven the adoption of digital banking.. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report: The Rise of Digital Banking in Personal Finance\nIntroduction\nThe financial landscape has undergone a profound transformation in the last decade, with digital banking emerging as a dominant force in personal finance management. This report explores the key drivers, benefits, and challenges of digital banking, and its impact on individuals’ financial habits.\n\nKey Drivers of Digital Banking Adoption\nTechnological Advancements\nThe proliferation of smartphones and high-speed internet has made banking services accessible anytime, anywhere. Enhanced security protocols, such as biometric authentication, have also increased user trust.\n\nChanging Consumer Expectations\nModern consumers demand convenience, speed, and transparency. Digital banks offer seamless user experiences, intuitive interfaces, and real-time notifications, aligning with these expectations.\n\nCost Efficiency\nDigital-only banks operate without physical branches, reducing overhead costs. These savings are often passed on to customers through lower fees and higher interest rates on deposits.\n\nBenefits for Personal Finance\n24/7 Access and Control\nUsers can check balances, transfer funds, and pay bills instantly, empowering them to manage their finances proactively.\n\nPersonalized Insights\nMany digital banks use AI to analyze spending patterns and provide tailored budgeting tips, helping users save more effectively.\n\nEnhanced Security\nFeatures like two-factor authentication, instant card freezing, and transaction alerts improve security and reduce fraud risk.\n\nChallenges and Considerations\nCybersecurity Risks\nAs digital banking grows, so do threats from hackers and scammers. Continuous investment in cybersecurity is essential.\n\nDigital Divide\nNot all populations have equal access to digital tools, potentially excluding vulnerable groups from the benefits of digital banking.\n\nRegulatory Compliance\nDigital banks must navigate complex regulations across different jurisdictions, balancing innovation with compliance.\n\nImpact on Financial Habits\nDigital banking encourages financial literacy by providing users with real-time data and educational resources. Automated savings features and spending categorizations help users set and achieve financial goals, fostering healthier financial behaviors.\n\nConclusion\nDigital banking is reshaping personal finance by making money management more accessible, efficient, and secure. While challenges remain, its continued evolution promises to further empower individuals in their financial journeys.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.00419158861041069}, {'seq_length': 35, 'latency': 0.35427261609584093, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: How do digital banks foster healthier financial behaviors?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report: The Rise of Digital Banking in Personal Finance\nIntroduction\nThe financial landscape has undergone a profound transformation in the last decade, with digital banking emerging as a dominant force in personal finance management. This report explores the key drivers, benefits, and challenges of digital banking, and its impact on individuals’ financial habits.\n\nKey Drivers of Digital Banking Adoption\nTechnological Advancements\nThe proliferation of smartphones and high-speed internet has made banking services accessible anytime, anywhere. Enhanced security protocols, such as biometric authentication, have also increased user trust.\n\nChanging Consumer Expectations\nModern consumers demand convenience, speed, and transparency. Digital banks offer seamless user experiences, intuitive interfaces, and real-time notifications, aligning with these expectations.\n\nCost Efficiency\nDigital-only banks operate without physical branches, reducing overhead costs. These savings are often passed on to customers through lower fees and higher interest rates on deposits.\n\nBenefits for Personal Finance\n24/7 Access and Control\nUsers can check balances, transfer funds, and pay bills instantly, empowering them to manage their finances proactively.\n\nPersonalized Insights\nMany digital banks use AI to analyze spending patterns and provide tailored budgeting tips, helping users save more effectively.\n\nEnhanced Security\nFeatures like two-factor authentication, instant card freezing, and transaction alerts improve security and reduce fraud risk.\n\nChallenges and Considerations\nCybersecurity Risks\nAs digital banking grows, so do threats from hackers and scammers. Continuous investment in cybersecurity is essential.\n\nDigital Divide\nNot all populations have equal access to digital tools, potentially excluding vulnerable groups from the benefits of digital banking.\n\nRegulatory Compliance\nDigital banks must navigate complex regulations across different jurisdictions, balancing innovation with compliance.\n\nImpact on Financial Habits\nDigital banking encourages financial literacy by providing users with real-time data and educational resources. Automated savings features and spending categorizations help users set and achieve financial goals, fostering healthier financial behaviors.\n\nConclusion\nDigital banking is reshaping personal finance by making money management more accessible, efficient, and secure. While challenges remain, its continued evolution promises to further empower individuals in their financial journeys.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004066267982125282}, {'seq_length': 40, 'latency': 1.0097589949145913, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What does the report suggest about the relevance of Leonardo’s legacy today?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report on Art: The Genius of Leonardo da Vinci\nIntroduction\nArt is a profound expression of human creativity, emotion, and intellect. Throughout history, artists have shaped our understanding of the world, challenging conventions and inspiring generations. Among the pantheon of great artists, Leonardo da Vinci stands out as a true polymath whose influence extends far beyond the canvas.\n\nLeonardo da Vinci: The Quintessential Renaissance Man\nLeonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian painter, sculptor, inventor, scientist, and engineer. His insatiable curiosity and inventive mind made him the embodiment of the Renaissance humanist ideal. Da Vinci’s art is renowned for its technical mastery, innovative composition, and deep humanity.\n\nArtistic Achievements\nThe Mona Lisa\nPerhaps the most famous painting in the world, the Mona Lisa captivates viewers with her enigmatic smile and lifelike presence. Leonardo’s mastery of sfumato—a technique of subtle gradation between colors and tones—creates a sense of depth and realism that was revolutionary for its time.\n\nThe Last Supper\nIn The Last Supper, Leonardo reimagined a biblical scene with dramatic perspective and psychological intensity. The painting’s composition draws the viewer’s eye to Christ at the center, while the apostles’ varied reactions convey a powerful narrative.\n\nScientific Approach\nLeonardo’s notebooks reveal a relentless quest to understand anatomy, light, and perspective. He dissected human bodies to study muscles and bones, applying this knowledge to achieve unprecedented realism in his art. His sketches, such as the Vitruvian Man, illustrate the harmony between art and science.\n\nLegacy\nLeonardo da Vinci’s influence is immeasurable. He bridged the gap between art and science, inspiring artists, engineers, and thinkers for centuries. His works continue to attract millions of admirers and provoke new interpretations.\n\nConclusion\nLeonardo da Vinci exemplifies the boundless potential of human creativity. His art, driven by curiosity and observation, remains a testament to the enduring power of imagination and intellect. For anyone passionate about the intersection of art, science, and innovation, Leonardo da Vinci is an artist whose legacy is as relevant today as it was five centuries ago.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.009878898970782757}, {'seq_length': 42, 'latency': 2.1593753276392817, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What is the central theme of Leonardo’s artistic achievements, according to the report?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report on Art: The Genius of Leonardo da Vinci\nIntroduction\nArt is a profound expression of human creativity, emotion, and intellect. Throughout history, artists have shaped our understanding of the world, challenging conventions and inspiring generations. Among the pantheon of great artists, Leonardo da Vinci stands out as a true polymath whose influence extends far beyond the canvas.\n\nLeonardo da Vinci: The Quintessential Renaissance Man\nLeonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian painter, sculptor, inventor, scientist, and engineer. His insatiable curiosity and inventive mind made him the embodiment of the Renaissance humanist ideal. Da Vinci’s art is renowned for its technical mastery, innovative composition, and deep humanity.\n\nArtistic Achievements\nThe Mona Lisa\nPerhaps the most famous painting in the world, the Mona Lisa captivates viewers with her enigmatic smile and lifelike presence. Leonardo’s mastery of sfumato—a technique of subtle gradation between colors and tones—creates a sense of depth and realism that was revolutionary for its time.\n\nThe Last Supper\nIn The Last Supper, Leonardo reimagined a biblical scene with dramatic perspective and psychological intensity. The painting’s composition draws the viewer’s eye to Christ at the center, while the apostles’ varied reactions convey a powerful narrative.\n\nScientific Approach\nLeonardo’s notebooks reveal a relentless quest to understand anatomy, light, and perspective. He dissected human bodies to study muscles and bones, applying this knowledge to achieve unprecedented realism in his art. His sketches, such as the Vitruvian Man, illustrate the harmony between art and science.\n\nLegacy\nLeonardo da Vinci’s influence is immeasurable. He bridged the gap between art and science, inspiring artists, engineers, and thinkers for centuries. His works continue to attract millions of admirers and provoke new interpretations.\n\nConclusion\nLeonardo da Vinci exemplifies the boundless potential of human creativity. His art, driven by curiosity and observation, remains a testament to the enduring power of imagination and intellect. For anyone passionate about the intersection of art, science, and innovation, Leonardo da Vinci is an artist whose legacy is as relevant today as it was five centuries ago.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.0042345840483903885}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 0.4567476836964488, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Who is described as the quintessential Renaissance man in the report?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: One-Page Report on Art: The Genius of Leonardo da Vinci\nIntroduction\nArt is a profound expression of human creativity, emotion, and intellect. Throughout history, artists have shaped our understanding of the world, challenging conventions and inspiring generations. Among the pantheon of great artists, Leonardo da Vinci stands out as a true polymath whose influence extends far beyond the canvas.\n\nLeonardo da Vinci: The Quintessential Renaissance Man\nLeonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian painter, sculptor, inventor, scientist, and engineer. His insatiable curiosity and inventive mind made him the embodiment of the Renaissance humanist ideal. Da Vinci’s art is renowned for its technical mastery, innovative composition, and deep humanity.\n\nArtistic Achievements\nThe Mona Lisa\nPerhaps the most famous painting in the world, the Mona Lisa captivates viewers with her enigmatic smile and lifelike presence. Leonardo’s mastery of sfumato—a technique of subtle gradation between colors and tones—creates a sense of depth and realism that was revolutionary for its time.\n\nThe Last Supper\nIn The Last Supper, Leonardo reimagined a biblical scene with dramatic perspective and psychological intensity. The painting’s composition draws the viewer’s eye to Christ at the center, while the apostles’ varied reactions convey a powerful narrative.\n\nScientific Approach\nLeonardo’s notebooks reveal a relentless quest to understand anatomy, light, and perspective. He dissected human bodies to study muscles and bones, applying this knowledge to achieve unprecedented realism in his art. His sketches, such as the Vitruvian Man, illustrate the harmony between art and science.\n\nLegacy\nLeonardo da Vinci’s influence is immeasurable. He bridged the gap between art and science, inspiring artists, engineers, and thinkers for centuries. His works continue to attract millions of admirers and provoke new interpretations.\n\nConclusion\nLeonardo da Vinci exemplifies the boundless potential of human creativity. His art, driven by curiosity and observation, remains a testament to the enduring power of imagination and intellect. For anyone passionate about the intersection of art, science, and innovation, Leonardo da Vinci is an artist whose legacy is as relevant today as it was five centuries ago.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.00398126058280468}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 1.319234044291079, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which statistical tests are commonly used by Evidently AI for drift detection?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Summary of "Open-Source Drift Detection Tools in Action: Insights from Two Use Cases" (Müller et al., 2024)\n\nThis paper addresses the critical challenge of data drift in machine learning (ML) systems, which can significantly degrade model performance and reliability when the statistical properties of production data diverge from those seen during training. To help practitioners select appropriate drift detection tools, the authors introduce D3Bench, a microbenchmark designed to evaluate the effectiveness of three leading open-source drift detection libraries—Evidently AI, NannyML, and Alibi-Detect—using real-world smart building datasets1.\n\nTypes of Data Drift and Benchmarking Approach\n\nThe authors categorize data drift into four main types: Covariate Drift, Prior Probability Drift, Concept Drift, and Dataset Shift. These can manifest as abrupt, incremental, or recurring (seasonal) changes. D3Bench evaluates both functional (accuracy, integrability, flexibility, user-friendliness) and non-functional (runtime, memory usage) aspects of the tools, using two real-world use cases:\n\nUC1 (Occupancy Detection): Involves time series data on room occupancy, CO₂ levels, and temperature, with a concept shift occurring due to a change in ventilation strategy.\n\nUC2 (Energy Consumption Prediction): Analyzes thermal energy usage and outside temperature for university buildings, with dataset shift observed over a multi-year period1.\n\nOverview of Evaluated Tools\n\nTool\tStrengths & Features\tWeaknesses & Limitations\nEvidently AI\tBroad suite of univariate drift detection methods; strong reporting and visualization; easy integration with MLOps tools; supports tabular and text data\tNo support for image data; limited time series-specific features\nNannyML\tPrecise drift timing; links drift to model accuracy; detailed visualizations; supports tabular data; integrates with Grafana\tLimited to tabular data; report generation is slow\nAlibi-Detect\tSupports tabular, image, time series, and text data; both offline and online detection; flexible backends\tSlower runtime; inconsistent results; limited reporting and guidance\nKey Findings from Use Cases\n\nEvidently AI excelled at detecting general data drifts in both input and target variables, providing comprehensive reports and smooth integration with ML workflows. It is particularly effective when only predicted values are available.\n\nNannyML was superior in pinpointing the exact timing of drifts and correlating these with drops in model accuracy, making it valuable for monitoring model performance in production.\n\nAlibi-Detect demonstrated broader data type support (including images) but suffered from slower runtimes and less user-friendly reporting. Its Spot-The-Difference test could not always localize drift to specific variables1.\n\nPerformance and Usability\n\nRuntime: Evidently AI and NannyML were significantly faster than Alibi-Detect, especially in large datasets. Report generation increased runtime, particularly for NannyML.\n\nMemory Usage: All tools had similar, modest memory footprints, with minor variations.\n\nUser Experience: Evidently AI and NannyML offered superior documentation and visualizations, aiding adoption and interpretation. Alibi-Detect required more manual setup and backend configuration1.\n\nRecommended Workflow\n\nThe authors propose a three-step process for robust drift management:\n\nAssess model accuracy to screen for possible drift (using built-in monitoring or NannyML).\n\nConfirm drift presence and affected variables with Evidently AI, leveraging its statistical tests and visualizations.\n\nPinpoint drift timing and impact on accuracy using NannyML for fine-grained analysis1.\n\nConclusion and Future Work\n\nThe study concludes that Evidently AI is the most versatile and user-friendly for general drift detection and integration into ML pipelines, while NannyML is preferred for detailed, time-resolved drift analysis and performance monitoring. Alibi-Detect is recommended when support for non-tabular data (e.g., images) is required. The authors note that their benchmark focuses on univariate detection, suggesting future work should explore multivariate and image-based drift detection methods to address more complex real-world scenarios'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004032447934150696}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 0.43686321564018726, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: Which prior systems does Metron build upon according to the related work section?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and dispatching and achieves superior throughput and lower latency than OpenBox and E2.\n\n2. System Architecture  \n2.1 Overview  \nMetron identifies traffic classes, synthesizes processing graphs, and uses programmable switches and NICs to implement stateless tasks. The rest is executed on designated CPU cores, using NIC-based tags for traffic steering.\n\n2.2 Data Plane  \nA master-slave architecture is adopted, where the master agent manages hardware bindings and delegates processing tasks to slaves. NICs dispatch packets directly to appropriate CPU cores, improving speed.\n\n2.3 Control Plane  \nGraph synthesis via SNF merges NF logic, while placement and dynamic scaling strategies minimize overhead. Traffic classes are used to scale NFs efficiently without duplicating logic unnecessarily.\n\n2.4 Routing and Failures  \nMetron installs tagged OpenFlow rules for routing and dispatching. Backup rules support fast failover, and controller updates switches and NICs during path changes.\n\n3. Evaluation  \n3.1 Large-Scale Deployment  \nMetron performs DPI at 40 Gbps and supports stateful service chains at 100 Gbps. It outperforms OpenBox and E2 by a large margin in both latency and throughput, using fewer CPU cores.\n\n3.2 Dynamic Scaling  \nMetron adapts to workload changes by tagging traffic classes and redirecting them across CPU cores, offering fast and fine-grained scaling with minimal latency.\n\n3.3 Deployment Micro-benchmarks  \nMetron installs thousands of rules within hundreds of milliseconds, using hardware/software combination. It handles diverse switch capabilities and supports deployments across large-scale topologies efficiently.\n\n4. Related Work  \nMetron builds upon ideas from E2, OpenBox, SNF, and modern NIC capabilities. Unlike others, it aggressively uses hardware offloading and intelligent placement for performance.\n\n5. Conclusion  \nMetron demonstrates that efficient NFV service chains can be realized on commodity servers by leveraging hardware-aware placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004007497802376747}, {'seq_length': 40, 'latency': 0.7146473303437233, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: How does Metron handle routing and failover in case of failures?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and dispatching and achieves superior throughput and lower latency than OpenBox and E2.\n\n2. System Architecture  \n2.1 Overview  \nMetron identifies traffic classes, synthesizes processing graphs, and uses programmable switches and NICs to implement stateless tasks. The rest is executed on designated CPU cores, using NIC-based tags for traffic steering.\n\n2.2 Data Plane  \nA master-slave architecture is adopted, where the master agent manages hardware bindings and delegates processing tasks to slaves. NICs dispatch packets directly to appropriate CPU cores, improving speed.\n\n2.3 Control Plane  \nGraph synthesis via SNF merges NF logic, while placement and dynamic scaling strategies minimize overhead. Traffic classes are used to scale NFs efficiently without duplicating logic unnecessarily.\n\n2.4 Routing and Failures  \nMetron installs tagged OpenFlow rules for routing and dispatching. Backup rules support fast failover, and controller updates switches and NICs during path changes.\n\n3. Evaluation  \n3.1 Large-Scale Deployment  \nMetron performs DPI at 40 Gbps and supports stateful service chains at 100 Gbps. It outperforms OpenBox and E2 by a large margin in both latency and throughput, using fewer CPU cores.\n\n3.2 Dynamic Scaling  \nMetron adapts to workload changes by tagging traffic classes and redirecting them across CPU cores, offering fast and fine-grained scaling with minimal latency.\n\n3.3 Deployment Micro-benchmarks  \nMetron installs thousands of rules within hundreds of milliseconds, using hardware/software combination. It handles diverse switch capabilities and supports deployments across large-scale topologies efficiently.\n\n4. Related Work  \nMetron builds upon ideas from E2, OpenBox, SNF, and modern NIC capabilities. Unlike others, it aggressively uses hardware offloading and intelligent placement for performance.\n\n5. Conclusion  \nMetron demonstrates that efficient NFV service chains can be realized on commodity servers by leveraging hardware-aware placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.00395254697650671}, {'seq_length': 38, 'latency': 0.532536038197577, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: How does Metron implement traffic steering using NIC-based tags?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and dispatching and achieves superior throughput and lower latency than OpenBox and E2.\n\n2. System Architecture  \n2.1 Overview  \nMetron identifies traffic classes, synthesizes processing graphs, and uses programmable switches and NICs to implement stateless tasks. The rest is executed on designated CPU cores, using NIC-based tags for traffic steering.\n\n2.2 Data Plane  \nA master-slave architecture is adopted, where the master agent manages hardware bindings and delegates processing tasks to slaves. NICs dispatch packets directly to appropriate CPU cores, improving speed.\n\n2.3 Control Plane  \nGraph synthesis via SNF merges NF logic, while placement and dynamic scaling strategies minimize overhead. Traffic classes are used to scale NFs efficiently without duplicating logic unnecessarily.\n\n2.4 Routing and Failures  \nMetron installs tagged OpenFlow rules for routing and dispatching. Backup rules support fast failover, and controller updates switches and NICs during path changes.\n\n3. Evaluation  \n3.1 Large-Scale Deployment  \nMetron performs DPI at 40 Gbps and supports stateful service chains at 100 Gbps. It outperforms OpenBox and E2 by a large margin in both latency and throughput, using fewer CPU cores.\n\n3.2 Dynamic Scaling  \nMetron adapts to workload changes by tagging traffic classes and redirecting them across CPU cores, offering fast and fine-grained scaling with minimal latency.\n\n3.3 Deployment Micro-benchmarks  \nMetron installs thousands of rules within hundreds of milliseconds, using hardware/software combination. It handles diverse switch capabilities and supports deployments across large-scale topologies efficiently.\n\n4. Related Work  \nMetron builds upon ideas from E2, OpenBox, SNF, and modern NIC capabilities. Unlike others, it aggressively uses hardware offloading and intelligent placement for performance.\n\n5. Conclusion  \nMetron demonstrates that efficient NFV service chains can be realized on commodity servers by leveraging hardware-aware placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.00396291259676218}, {'seq_length': 37, 'latency': 1.2615293879061937, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What advanced queueing technique provides fairness in bandwidth allocation?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Metron: NFV Service Chains at the True Speed of the Underlying Hardware  \nAuthors: Georgios P. Katsikas, Tom Barbette, Dejan Kostić, Rebecca Steinert, Gerald Q. Maguire Jr.\n\nAbstract  \nMetron is an NFV platform that fully utilizes commodity server and network resources to run stateful service chains efficiently. It achieves zero inter-core communication using smart tagging and hardware dispatching, attaining up to 100 Gbps performance on a single server.\n\n1. Introduction  \nNFV seeks to replace hardware middleboxes with software NFs. While beneficial, it faces performance challenges. Metron eliminates packet handovers between cores using hardware tagging and dispatching and achieves superior throughput and lower latency than OpenBox and E2.\n\n2. System Architecture  \n2.1 Overview  \nMetron identifies traffic classes, synthesizes processing graphs, and uses programmable switches and NICs to implement stateless tasks. The rest is executed on designated CPU cores, using NIC-based tags for traffic steering.\n\n2.2 Data Plane  \nA master-slave architecture is adopted, where the master agent manages hardware bindings and delegates processing tasks to slaves. NICs dispatch packets directly to appropriate CPU cores, improving speed.\n\n2.3 Control Plane  \nGraph synthesis via SNF merges NF logic, while placement and dynamic scaling strategies minimize overhead. Traffic classes are used to scale NFs efficiently without duplicating logic unnecessarily.\n\n2.4 Routing and Failures  \nMetron installs tagged OpenFlow rules for routing and dispatching. Backup rules support fast failover, and controller updates switches and NICs during path changes.\n\n3. Evaluation  \n3.1 Large-Scale Deployment  \nMetron performs DPI at 40 Gbps and supports stateful service chains at 100 Gbps. It outperforms OpenBox and E2 by a large margin in both latency and throughput, using fewer CPU cores.\n\n3.2 Dynamic Scaling  \nMetron adapts to workload changes by tagging traffic classes and redirecting them across CPU cores, offering fast and fine-grained scaling with minimal latency.\n\n3.3 Deployment Micro-benchmarks  \nMetron installs thousands of rules within hundreds of milliseconds, using hardware/software combination. It handles diverse switch capabilities and supports deployments across large-scale topologies efficiently.\n\n4. Related Work  \nMetron builds upon ideas from E2, OpenBox, SNF, and modern NIC capabilities. Unlike others, it aggressively uses hardware offloading and intelligent placement for performance.\n\n5. Conclusion  \nMetron demonstrates that efficient NFV service chains can be realized on commodity servers by leveraging hardware-aware placement, dispatching, and offloading strategies. It significantly outperforms state-of-the-art systems in both throughput and latency.\n\n6. Acknowledgments  \nThanks to funding from the Swedish Foundation for Strategic Research and WASP.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.0038814255967736244}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 1.4379464434459805, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: What visual indicator correlates with higher GPU utilization in Crux's flow patterns?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004264304414391518}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 0.5214378451928496, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: How does Crux differ fundamentally from job-level schedulers like HiveD?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004243082366883755}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 0.5054645305499434, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: How might fairness concerns in Crux's priority system be addressed?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.003973040729761124}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 0.5417167088016868, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What are the key differences in memory usage among the evaluated tools?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.003990913741290569}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 0.5268677789717913, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: What are the three primary memory challenges in LLM serving identified?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.004000863991677761}, {'seq_length': 41, 'latency': 0.7931161783635616, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': "User prompt: What OS concept inspired the design of vLLM's KV cache manager?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements."}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.0040070973336696625}, {'seq_length': 39, 'latency': 1.7019181521609426, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'User prompt: How does vLLM handle memory allocation for variable-length sequences?. \nPlease, answer given the following context: Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention  \nAuthors: Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody Hao Yu, Joseph E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, Ion Stoica\n\nAbstract  \nvLLM introduces PagedAttention, a novel attention mechanism inspired by virtual memory paging, enabling efficient key-value (KV) cache management for large language model (LLM) serving. This allows for near-zero memory waste and flexible KV cache sharing. Built atop PagedAttention, vLLM achieves up to 4× throughput improvements over FasterTransformer and Orca, especially with large models and complex decoding.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM serving is costly and memory-bound. The main bottleneck is managing dynamic KV caches. Existing systems allocate contiguous memory, resulting in internal and external fragmentation. vLLM proposes PagedAttention to manage KV cache more like OS paging, improving batching and reducing cost.\n\n2. Background  \nCovers Transformers, KV cache role in autoregressive generation, and batching challenges. Batching must handle asynchronous arrivals and sequence length variance. Iteration-level scheduling helps reduce queueing and padding inefficiencies.\n\n3. Memory Challenges in LLM Serving  \nIdentifies three major memory issues in LLMs:  \n- Large KV cache per request  \n- Inability to share cache across outputs or sequences  \n- Fragmentation due to static, contiguous allocation  \nThese lead to inefficient GPU memory usage and limit throughput.\n\n4. Method  \n4.1 PagedAttention  \nEnables KV cache to be divided into small, fixed-size blocks (KV blocks), stored non-contiguously.\n\n4.2 KV Cache Manager  \nInspired by OS virtual memory: separates logical vs physical KV blocks using block tables, supports dynamic allocation.\n\n4.3 Decoding  \nDemonstrates how KV cache grows with decoding and how vLLM avoids pre-reservation using paged logic.\n\n4.4 Decoding Scenarios  \nSupports complex tasks like parallel sampling and beam search with memory reuse and copy-on-write. Shared prefixes are handled efficiently.\n\n4.5 Scheduling and Preemption  \nImplements FCFS scheduling and preemption strategies using block eviction. Supports both swapping (CPU RAM) and recomputation as recovery mechanisms.\n\n4.6 Distributed Execution  \nvLLM supports model parallelism (e.g., Megatron-LM) by using a centralized KV cache manager and SPMD logic.\n\n5. Implementation  \nImplemented using FastAPI and PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels. Supports various LLMs (GPT, OPT, LLaMA). Memory access is fused with attention computation for efficiency.\n\n6. Evaluation  \nvLLM achieves 2–4× throughput vs. FasterTransformer/Orca. Outperforms in basic, parallel, beam, and shared prefix decoding. Key findings:\n- Handles more batched requests\n- Memory savings up to 66% in beam search\n- Strong performance on chatbots and large models\n\n7. Ablation Studies  \n- Kernel Overhead: Small latency tradeoff (~20%) vs. major memory gains  \n- Block Size: 16 is optimal for balancing GPU use and fragmentation  \n- Recovery: Recomputation preferred for small block sizes; swapping scales with size\n\n8. Discussion  \nPagedAttention may not apply to compute-bound workloads, but is ideal for memory-bound LLM serving. Its kernel fusion techniques make the overhead manageable.\n\n9. Related Work  \nBuilds on prior systems like Orca, FasterTransformer, DeepSpeed, FlashAttention, and memory optimization techniques like FlexGen and OLLA.\n\n10. Conclusion  \nvLLM introduces paging-inspired memory management to LLM serving. PagedAttention enables non-contiguous memory allocation and KV sharing, leading to substantial throughput improvements.'}], 'rag_accuracy': 0.8333333333333334, 'rag_latency': 0.003965326584875584}]
